Actually the cleanest: use `done` with 2 permits held by workers. Workers acquire their permit at the very start (before work), then release after work. Supervisor acquires 2 permits after joining — but joining already guarantees completion, so acquiring 2 permits will succeed immediately since workers released them. This is deadlock-free and uses the semaphore meaningfully.

Let me write it so the supervisor's acquire genuinely waits: workers acquire at start, supervisor tries to acquire 2. If supervisor runs first, it blocks until workers release. If workers run first, they hold permits, supervisor blocks until release. Either way it terminates. But if supervisor acquires before workers start, it gets permits immediately and doesn't wait — however `join()` still ensures correctness.

To make the semaphore the actual wait mechanism and guarantee termination, I'll have workers acquire at start and release at end, and supervisor acquire 2 permits (which only succeeds after both released). Combined with join for safety.

